{"category":"SUBSTANTIVE_MISMATCH","confidence":"MEDIUM","quotations":[{"turn_id":"T004","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"And when I'm out with the lads, or down the pub like we were talking about — you're not ordering a salad, are you? You're having a pie, a burger, something like that. That's what men do, that's what you expect."},{"turn_id":"T004","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"You get into habits early on — certain foods feel masculine, certain foods feel a bit... I don't know, fussy? And you just stick with that because that's what you know."},{"turn_id":"T007","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"But I've been eating the same way for over seventy years now. It's not just a habit, is it? It's... it's who I am, sort of."},{"turn_id":"T007","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"it feels like giving up something that matters, even if I can't quite explain why"},{"turn_id":"T012","speaker":"Participant 4","quote":"It's that I don't have a reason to. I'm not unhappy with how things are. I'm not straining against it."},{"turn_id":"T006","speaker":"Participant 3","quote":"And I stopped caring quite as much about whether it looked "manly" or whatever."}],"reasoning":"The reference theme makes a claim about the existence and normalisation of implicit masculine expectations: certain foods are coded as masculine, others as 'fussy', and men absorb and stop questioning these expectations. Its evidence is squarely the T004 material about pub food, 'that's what men do', and unexamined habits formed early.

The two candidate themes make different claims. The first is about identity-fused habits generating resistance to change ('it's who I am, sort of'; changing would feel like 'giving up something that matters') — the driver named is duration and personal identity, not gender norms; the word 'men' or 'masculine' does not appear in its evidence. The second is about the absence of any felt reason to change when one is content ('I don't have a reason to. I'm not unhappy with how things are') — a motivational/satisfaction claim that is orthogonal to whether masculine expectations exist or are normalised.

These are not finer-grained mechanisms of the reference claim; they are answers to a different question (why people do or don't change diet) drawn largely from a later stretch of the conversation (T007, T012) that the reference does not cite. There is a thin adjacency — Participant 4 does elsewhere say 'meat and potatoes, that's what you ate, that's what men did', and Participant 3 speaks of no longer caring whether food looked 'manly' — so an argument for a shared underlying topic of internalised norms could be made. But on the substance of the claims as stated and evidenced, they diverge rather than decompose, which is why this is a mismatch rather than a granularity difference. Confidence is MEDIUM because a broader reading of the reference (normalised expectations that become self-perpetuating) could plausibly encompass the identity-fusion candidate.","information_that_would_settle_it":"Whether the reference theme, as originally scoped, was intended to cover only the gendered coding of food (masculine vs 'fussy' foods and the expectations around them) or more broadly the unexamined persistence of ingrained eating patterns. Access to the reference theme's full evidence set — in particular whether it also drew on T007 or T012 — would settle it."}

{"category":"SUBSTANTIVE_MISMATCH","confidence":"MEDIUM","quotations":[{"turn_id":"T007","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"But I've been eating the same way for over seventy years now. It's not just a habit, is it? It's... it's who I am, sort of."},{"turn_id":"T007","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"It feels... I don't know, it feels like giving up something that matters, even if I can't quite explain why."},{"turn_id":"T004","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"certain foods feel masculine, certain foods feel a bit... I don't know, fussy? And you just stick with that because that's what you know. That's what feels normal for you."},{"turn_id":"T012","speaker":"Participant 4","quote":"It's that I don't have a reason to. I'm not unhappy with how things are. I'm not straining against it."},{"turn_id":"T008","speaker":"Participant 3","quote":"there's a difference, isn't there, between being stuck in something because you haven't questioned it, and staying with something because it actually matters to you"}],"reasoning":"The candidate makes one tightly bounded claim, grounded in T007: Patrick's decades-old eating pattern has fused with his sense of self, so changing it feels like surrendering part of who he is. That claim is clearly present in the extract and is a single coherent idea, not a bundle of separate claims, so this is not over-merging on the candidate's side.

The three reference themes it has been linked to do not decompose that claim into finer mechanisms; they make different claims about different material. The first is about eating decisions grounded in bodily/health perception or personal belief, evidenced by Toby's contentment ("It doesn't feel like a prison, and it doesn't feel like I'm missing out either")  or stisting change is exactly the opposite framing from the candidate's: Toby explicitly says his non-change is about having no reason, not about identity being at stake. The second is about implicit, normalised masculine expectations (pie not salad, wife plates the veg) 	a claim about social norms rather than about identity-fusion blocking change. The third is labelled as backlash or demands to reaffirm masculinity when one steps outside the norm; its cited evidence (Keith distinguishing being stuck from valuing something) does not show any backlash or demand at all, and is at best tangentially adjacent to the candidate's point.

So the candidate's specific claim 	identity fusion as the barrier to change 	is not the claim any of the three references is making, and the three together do not constitute a broader version of it. The relationship reads as themes about neighbouring but genuinely different content being tied together. Confidence is MEDIUM rather than HIGH because the third reference's evidence quote sits in the same stretch of talk as the candidate's evidence, and one could argue a loose thematic family around 'holding onto masculine food habits' that partly encompasses the candidate.","information_that_would_settle_it":"The coding conventions or codebook definitions behind the reference themes 	in particular whether the third theme ('stepping outside the norm leads to being questioned') is intended to cover internal resistance and identity threat as well as external backlash, and whether the first theme's 'personal beliefs' is meant to include identity-based reasons for not changing. Without those definitions, the mismatch is judged on the surface claims and evidence alone."}

{"category":"VALID_NOVEL_THEME","confidence":"MEDIUM","quotations":[{"turn_id":"T005","speaker":"Participant 1","quote":"I'm old enough not to care about that sort of thing. But I get why younger blokes might give someone grief over it."},{"turn_id":"T006","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"once you're past that — once you're forty, you've got your own life sorted, you don't need to prove anything — you just don't care. You eat what you want and nobody makes it weird."},{"turn_id":"T006","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"It's this idea that you have to defend it, or that there's some kind of... test you have to pass to prove you're still one of the lads or whatever. That feels immature, you know? Like, we're not kids anymore."}],"reasoning":"The candidate's claim — that age/maturity reduces both susceptibility to and participation in food-related conformity pressure — is directly and explicitly stated by both participants. Participant 1 contrasts himself ('old enough not to care') with 'younger blokes' who 'might give someone grief', and Participant 2 frames the policing as 'immature', 'we're not kids anymore', and says that by forty 'you don't need to prove anything — you just don't care'. This is a life-stage/maturity claim, not merely an incidental aside; it is voiced by both speakers.

None of the reference themes make this claim. The closest, 'STEPPING OUTSIDE THE NORM LEADS TO BEING QUESTIONED...', cites an evidence passage that happens to contain the age remark, but the theme itself asserts that norm-violation triggers backlash and demands to reaffirm masculinity — the opposite side of the point. The other reference themes concern women being more judged, men not worrying about food, eating what you crave, and implicit masculine conformity pressure; none states that maturity attenuates it.

Among the other candidate themes, 'Indifference diffuses food teasing' is the nearest neighbour, but its mechanism is different: it claims teasing fails when the target does not react (the rugby-playing vegetarian mate), which is about individual demeanour rather than age. The candidate here concerns ageing/maturity changing both one's own indifference and one's willingness to police others. The overlap is partial rather than a restatement, which is why I hold at MEDIUM rather than HIGH.","information_that_would_settle_it":"Nothing further is needed to establish support — the extract explicitly states the age/maturity claim in T005 and T006. The only residual question is a coding-convention one: whether the theme set treats 'indifference defuses teasing' and 'maturity reduces caring about conformity' as one theme or two. Knowing the granularity convention used by whoever produced these themes would settle that."}

{"category":"VALID_NOVEL_THEME","confidence":"MEDIUM","quotations":[{"turn_id":"T005","speaker":"Participant 1","quote":"And I think that's why I wouldn't bat an eye at a mate going vegetarian now — I'm old enough not to care about that sort of thing. But I get why younger blokes might give someone grief over it."},{"turn_id":"T006","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"once you're past that — once you're forty, you've got your own life sorted, you don't need to prove anything — you just don't care. You eat what you want and nobody makes it weird."},{"turn_id":"T006","speaker":"Participant 2","quote":"That feels immature, you know? Like, we're not kids anymore."}],"reasoning":"The claim — that age/maturity reduces susceptibility to, and participation in, group policing of food choices — is directly and explicitly stated by both participants. Participant 1 contrasts himself ("old enough not to care") with "younger blokes" who "might give someone grief", and Participant 2 frames the pressure as "immature" and says that by forty "you just don't care". This is not an incidental aside; it is the closing point each speaker makes about the dynamic.

None of the listed reference themes makes this claim: they concern backlash against norm violation, the subtle normalisation of masculine expectations, men not worrying about food, simplicity of eating what you want, and women being judged more. The reference theme on norm-policing quotes text that happens to contain the 'once you're forty' line, but its stated claim is about group pushback against deviance, not about an age/maturity gradient.

Among the other candidate themes, the closest is "Indifference diffuses food teasing", but that makes a different claim: that teasing fails when the target is unbothered (the rugby-playing vegetarian). The maturity theme is about a life-stage change in both giving and receiving such judgement. They are adjacent and share a psychological logic, so a reading that treats them as one idea is defensible — hence MEDIUM rather than HIGH.","information_that_would_settle_it":"A clearer statement of the coding convention for how finely related ideas should be separated — specifically whether 'not caring because you're mature/older' and 'teasing fades when the target is unbothered' are to be treated as one theme or two — would settle the residual overlap with the 'Indifference diffuses food teasing' candidate. On support and on distinctness from the reference list, the extract itself is sufficient."}
